From 0cc4e34e99898226fe66e30e807ffc314af8ac4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:49:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/13] fix: stop config regeneration destroying the config
 header and re-adding dead models
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The header-destruction root cause was the anchor, not the write:
config.find('models:') matched the literal "/v1/models:" inside config.yaml's
own header comment on line 51, before the real top-level key on line 144.
The preserved "header" was 3059 of 28005 bytes, so every run deleted the
entire providers: section — all 13 provider URLs and api_key_env entries.
Anchor on (?m)^models: instead, and find the block end as the next top-level
key.

Harden the write path while there:
  - safe by default: no --write means it only reports the diff
  - validate_config_text() refuses to write if a provider lost its url, a
    protected top-level key vanished, or a profile ends up empty
  - config.yaml.bak, then temp file + fsync + os.replace, so a crash cannot
    leave a truncated config

Drop provably-dead opencode endpoints (19 across the chains). The key
authenticates (GET /zen/v1/models returns 200) but 11 of its 12 models return
403 FreeTierError, paid big-pickle included; the attribution headers do not
change it. fetch_opencode now probes each candidate with a real 1-token call
and emits only what answers 200, so the nightly sync cannot re-add them, and
SKIP_OPENCODE_PROBE now shouts and tags output verified:false. The
auto-fallback is no longer picked by a kilocode-vs-opencode count, which had
made the guaranteed-403 big-pickle the last-resort endpoint of the `smart`
chain.

Remove the unroutable `test` profile: LogicalModels is [smart work fast
large], so it parsed fine but /v1/chat/completions always answered
"Unknown model".

The 06:00 sync runs maki with scripts/sync-instruction.txt rather than
regenerate_config.py, so that instruction is the real source of truth and
needed the same two rules. validate-config.py now fails on an extra profile
or a dead opencode model, so a bad nightly result is rejected before it
lands. Both guards were tested by injecting each defect.
