From b097b1f3c91c685d5b376fa46c8ea25ef778b043 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:06:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/13] fix: never truncate or double-terminate an SSE stream

A client stops parsing at the first `data: [DONE]`, so the sentinel must be
the last event and must appear exactly once. Two bugs violated that, both
found by asserting on captured streams rather than eyeballing them.

1. `[DONE]` emitted first, truncating the whole response.
   kilocode/dots-studio sends role-only deltas with `content:""` plus a
   `reasoning` field, so sseEventIsRelease never fired on a "real" token.
   The stream stayed buffered until `[DONE]` itself satisfied the release
   condition, which put the sentinel into the buffer that the post-loop
   flush wrote ahead of its own. The response began with a stray `[DONE]`
   and every real chunk after it was dropped by the client, so `smart` and
   `work` intermittently returned nothing. Upstream's own stream was
   well-formed, confirming the fault was ours.

2. `[DONE]` duplicated. With `-tool-calls` disabled (the shipped default)
   the write path forwards events verbatim, so upstream's sentinel went out
   and the post-loop one followed.

`streamSSE` now intercepts `[DONE]` unconditionally, flushes the pending
buffer in order, and emits the single terminating sentinel itself. The
toolCalls branch no longer needs its own isDone guard.

Also skip SSE comment lines. commandcode2's relay interleaves `: keepalive`
between events; the scanner appended it to eventBuf, so it was dispatched as
a pseudo-event, sseEventIsRelease failed open on it (releasing the stream at
an arbitrary point), and the comment leaked to the client. The SSE spec says
to ignore them.

Regression tests (verified failing on the old code):
  TestStreamSSE_DoneIsAlwaysLastAndEmittedOnce
  TestStreamSSE_IgnoresSSECommentLines
  TestStreamSSE_EmitsDoneWhenUpstreamOmitsIt

Live: 20/20 clean across all four profiles, then a 30-request randomized
soak; content reassembles correctly and non-streaming is unaffected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
