From 9a6485dc5684f2a46cd7411ec9367e6a71610d56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:00:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/13] test: rename stale SelectNext tests, add secret scan
 and SSE negative check
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The two tests named after Router.SelectNext — a symbol deleted several rounds
ago — were still calling SelectEndpoint. The name outlived the symbol and sent
readers looking for something that no longer exists; renamed to
TestRouterSelectEndpointSkipsCooldown and
TestRouterSelectEndpointAllInCooldown, with the discrepancy documented at the
call site and the audit entry corrected to say so.

Two guards that were previously manual and one-off:

- scripts/secret-scan.sh checks tracked content, recent history, and
  .git/config. A GitHub PAT was found embedded in the origin remote URL: never
  committed, but in cleartext on disk, in every `git remote -v`, and in the
  process table of every push. Detections are reported by file and line with
  the token withheld, because printing it would turn a detection into a second
  exposure in a retained CI log. Covers gh*/github_pat_/glpat-/AKIA/xox* and
  private-key headers; no false positives on api_key_env or CI key fixtures.

- scripts/sse-negative-check.sh proves the SSE ordering tests have teeth: it
  reintroduces the historical bug, requires the tests to fail, restores, then
  requires them to pass and the file to be byte-identical to HEAD. Exits 2 if
  streamSSE is refactored past the mutation site, so a legitimate refactor
  produces a warning rather than a silent pass. Restores via trap.

Both run in the go CI job.

docs/audit.md: every entry header now leads with the function symbol and keeps
the historical line range in parentheses. 31 findings converted; the statuses
themselves are unchanged.
