From 5f9a6fa8cba4b117be84aa2bf8d31a8bbb8b4b53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:05:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/13] test: add audit anchor drift check and harden the SSE
 negative check

The audit doc was converted from line numbers to symbol anchors, but nothing
enforced it, so "use the symbol, not the line" was a convention that would rot
exactly the way the line numbers did. scripts/audit-drift-check.py resolves every
anchor against the Go sources and fails on one that no longer exists, with
ALLOWLIST/SPECIAL escape hatches that each require a stated reason. Verified it
fails on an injected bogus anchor and passes on the real doc.

sse-negative-check.sh had two defects that made it weaker than it looked:

  - It grepped for the mutation site and then ran a second python block to
    mutate it. Two copies of the same site text can drift apart, and when they
    do the pre-check reports "refactored" for a site that is still present. The
    python block is now the single source of truth, and its exit code is
    captured directly (`if ! cmd` would have captured the negation, not the
    command).
  - It confirmed the mutation with a substring grep, but the broken form is a
    prefix of the fixed form, so the grep matched the unmutated file too. It now
    compares against the backup, which is the only real test.

Also distinguish exit 3 (site gone, a refactor warning) from a write failure, so
a future maintainer is not told their guard is broken when streamSSE merely
moved.
