From d04f86df179807f863ac248044d178367af92548 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:21:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/13] fix: bound metrics label cardinality, escape label
 values, drop key leakage
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/metrics was mounted four commits ago and had never been exercised against
hostile or merely churning input. Three defects, all in metrics.go.

Unbounded label cardinality. Endpoint and model labels come from config, and
config is not fixed for the process lifetime: HandleAdminConfig accepts an
arbitrary new config and watchConfig hot-reloads the file every 3s. Nothing
evicted a series, and each endpoint cost four counters plus a 12-bucket
histogram. Measured before the fix: 5000 distinct labels produced 5000 entries
and a 6.9 MB scrape. A config churn loop — or a nightly model sync that keeps
inventing model ids — grows the process until it is OOM-killed, so the metrics
endpoint was a way to kill the gateway. Every label map is now capped at
maxLabelValues (512, ~8x the 60 endpoint keys in the live config); unknown
values collapse into __overflow__ and still get counted, and
airouter_metrics_label_overflow_total makes the condition visible instead of
silent. Already-tracked labels stay distinct, so a churning config never
displaces the endpoints that are actually hot.

Label values were also interpolated into the quoted exposition string with no
escaping, so a provider named `x" 1\nairouter_requests_total{model="victim`
forged a second, syntactically valid series that an operator reads as genuine.
labelEscape now escapes backslash, quote, and newline at every site. The
regression test uses a real exposition-format parser rather than eyeballing
the output, and the matching CI step was verified to reject both an unescaped
forgery and a raw-newline injection, so it is not decorative.

Label values were also storage keys leaking into the output: the store was
keyed by "requests_by_model_" + model and that composite was interpolated
straight into the label, so every series read model="requests_by_model_smart".
Cosmetic alone, but it forced every dashboard query to hard-code a storage
detail, which made the series undiscoverable.

Two implementation notes, because both were bugs the tests caught rather than
review. Bounding each map independently inside lazyCounter was wrong twice
over: the bounded value never propagated back to the caller, so the histogram
map was still keyed by the raw label and reached 6400 entries while the counter
maps stopped at 512; and per-map checks could disagree at the boundary, giving
an endpoint attempt counts with no latency histogram. Deciding once in
boundedAttemptKey and threading that key through all four maps makes the key
sets identical by construction. Separately, the first version let the map reach
cap+1 by admitting __overflow__ on top of cap real endpoints; a limit that is
not actually the limit is worse than no limit, so one slot is now reserved.

go vet also caught a latent conversion bug found while escaping: CircuitState
is an int-backed enum, so string(from) emitted the rune for the state number
instead of closed/open/half-open. Now uses CircuitState.String.

CI: every job sets continue-on-error: false and fail-fast: false, so a red X
is a failure rather than a "known flake" and one broken guard does not cancel
the others. Added a step that parses /metrics from the booted gateway and
rejects control characters and unquoted label values. Branch protection is an
org-level setting and cannot be set from the repo, so docs/RUNBOOK.md now
documents the exact required status checks to register, and notes that leaving
sse-contract out of that list makes a red X advisory.
