From a0daa560db0be67cb019bf41875a20faa5ad8d73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:43:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] fix: recycle metrics labels instead of starving, fix
 malformed overflow sample
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The cardinality cap from d04f86d held the line but starved: it admitted the
first 512 distinct labels and refused everything else, permanently. Config is
hot-reloadable, so labels from a dead config held their slots for the life of
the process, and a newly hot endpoint appearing later was collapsed into
__overflow__ forever — the gateway would report a broken upstream as "no data"
indefinitely, which is the worst possible failure for the series whose job is
answering "is this upstream healthy?".

attemptsByEndpoint now recycles: an untracked label at a full window evicts the
least-recently-admitted tracked endpoint and rolls its attempt count, failure
count and latency sum into the bucket. FIFO by admission, not LRU — refreshing
position on every touch would take the global write lock on every observation.
Per-bucket histogram data is deliberately not merged and cannot honestly be;
the RUNBOOK says so explicitly so nobody later "fixes" it.

That was strictly worse than the bug it fixed. The maps bound labels in two
shapes: attemptsByEndpoint keys on a bare value, but circuitTransitions keys on
a pre-rendered label set that the exporter splices in unquoted. Its overflow
key is a bare word where a label set belongs, so hitting the cap emitted
`airouter_circuit_state_transitions{__overflow__} 4` — not a legal sample. A
Prometheus scrape is all-or-nothing, so that would have taken down every
dashboard, alert and recording rule on the instance. The bound is worthless if
the price of reaching it is losing the metrics. The bucket now renders as
{endpoint="__overflow__"}.

Every prior test passed while that was broken, because they asserted on map
contents and substrings rather than parsing the output. New tests assert on
parsed samples and were each verified to fail against the unfixed code first.

Also fixed along the way:
  - Attempt did admission and counter update under separate locks, so a
    concurrent eviction could remove a key in between and the in-flight
    observation would recreate it, exceeding the cap and double-counting.
  - The overflow bucket was in the recycling ring, reporting one eviction as
    two; it is now pinned outside.
  - The rollup read-then-maybe-wrote, dropping failure and latency history
    for the first label of its kind — i.e. every endpoint that failed on its
    only attempt, precisely the ones worth knowing about.
  - Config.validate dereferenced the nil *Preferences, panicking on any config
    without a preferences: block and taking the suite with it.

The cap is now preferences.max_label_cardinality, applied at construction and
on every reload, and rejected rather than clamped when out of range: below the
floor destroys the series the cap exists to protect, above the ceiling defeats
the memory bound, and a setting that silently does something other than what
it says is worse than a startup failure. validate-config.py enforces the same
bounds before a restart, so TestValidateConfigRangeMatchesGo parses the script
to keep the two from drifting.

FuzzExpositionRoundTrip replaces the hand-picked table of nasty label strings
with a property test: for any value, what labelEscape writes is what the parser
reads back. 174M execs, no counterexample.

250 race tests pass; gofmt, vet, secret scan and audit drift check clean; SSE
contract verified 12/12 against a live gateway, and the new CI label-shape step
verified to catch the malformed line it exists for.
