From 7a34f234aa96f0c94d3ed61df5ff5872d52fe95e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fra <fra@localhost>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:58:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] fix: remove the two-key-convention bug class, bound the
 status maps
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Round 2 fixed the `{__overflow__}` malformed sample with a special case in one
exporter. That is the wrong shape of fix for a defect that comes from a
convention, so this removes the convention.

The maps did not agree on what a label value is. Most keyed on a bare value
and escaped at export; circuitTransitions keyed on a pre-rendered label set
that the exporter spliced in raw. The second shape means the map key is a
whole label SET, so a single-value overflow bucket no longer fits the key
space. Nothing recorded which convention applied where, so a new map would pick
one at random and the only symptom would be a scrape that fails to parse, in
production, from the cap that exists to make things safer. Every bounded map
now keys on a labelKey and is written by one helper, so the bucket is a legal
key everywhere and the exporter has no special case left to forget.

Writing the table test that asserts this for all six maps immediately found two
more real defects, both of which two rounds of cardinality work had missed:

  counterStatus and counterFailure were never actually bounded. They had the
  key type, the cap, and a comment claiming they were bounded — but no call
  enforcing it. The status is resp.StatusCode forwarded from the UPSTREAM, so
  an upstream varying its status per request drives unbounded labels into both
  maps: 640 distinct labels against a cap of 16, measured. The same
  self-inflicted DoS the rest of the file prevents, still open, invisible to
  every prior test because they all asserted on endpoints and models.

  All five collapsing counters decided the key under a read lock, released it,
  then inserted under a write lock — so N goroutines with N new labels could
  all see "there is room" and all insert, reaching cap+N. The memory bound
  silently did not apply under exactly the load that generates the most labels.
  Now one write lock for re-check, decision and insert, with a read-lock fast
  path for keys already tracked.

Also:
  bareKey used %q on an already-escaped value. %q is Go string quoting, which
  re-escapes the backslashes, so every label containing a quote or newline came
  out double-escaped: still valid, still safe, still parsing, but no longer
  matching the configured value, so the series could never be joined to config.
  Silent corruption that every existing check passed, including the round-trip
  fuzzer — a double escape unescapes to the original. The fuzz target now
  compares labelEscape against referenceEscape, an independent naive escaper in
  the test file; substring heuristics on escaping flagged every correct seed
  and the fuzzer produced a false positive, which is the argument for an oracle
  over a guess.

  Collapsing maps all collapsed into endpoint="__overflow__", including the
  status maps. That changes the metric's label schema, and Prometheus reads a
  label-set change as a new series, so a query grouping by status would lose the
  bucket entirely. Each map now collapses under its own label name.

Every new test was verified to fail against the unfixed code first, including
the concurrency one — which samples len() while workers run, because the
overshoot is what remains at the end and an after-the-fact assertion passes
against the bug. Its first version read len() without the lock and the race
detector caught that before it ever reached the product code.

Adds a README with the config reference; the previous one had drifted on the
jitter key name and half-open probe default, which is the kind of thing a
config reference that is never checked against the code always does.

260 race tests pass; gofmt, vet, secret scan and audit drift check clean; SSE
contract verified 12/12 against a live gateway.
